From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:11 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 18/21] dndcp: Release a native drag only once the target
 accepts it

The button must be released on the target's current answer. A Gtk target
answers one pointer motion with one accept and two refusals. The host
sends motions about 1.2 ms apart and releases as soon as it stops, so
the last answer is wherever the target had got to, and about a third of
the time that is a refusal. Before releasing, wait for an accept that
stays current for 30 ms, for at most 500 ms per attempt. A target that
refuses still ends the drag when the wait runs out. The block on the
staged files goes in after the wait, since the target stats the files to
decide.

A file manager fetches the offer's contents and decides only on a motion
that comes after they arrive. When the pointer has already stopped, move
it one pixel and back. Thunar can also carry the contents of one drag
into the next, when the pointer crossed its window faster than it kept
up. It then refuses the next drag on files that are gone, without
looking at the new offer, until it sees a leave. A short-lived overlay
on each output takes the drag off Thunar and gives it back. The drag's
own overlay cannot be used for that, because an unmapped layer surface
returns to the unconfigured state, and KWin never configures it again.

Measured with a host driving real drags on sway 1.11, files land in
Thunar 4.20.9 and PCManFM, the target settles in 32 to 43 ms, and a drop
onto no target is discarded. Host drags into Dolphin 26.08.1 on KDE
Plasma 6.7.5 were tested by hand. In nested compositors, start_drag from
a faked press serial and delivery to a native client also work on KWin
6.7.5 and Mutter 50.5, and all three compositors refuse a
wl_pointer.enter serial. The retry of WaylandDrag_Init() and the XDND
fallback after a failed start have not been tested on a VM.
